{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\nOperator directive (verbatim, 2026-08-13): 'add standing guidance for the project: flaky tests get debugged using rebar-debug, not retried until they pass. a flaky CI pipeline is a failure that slows development, wastes tokens, and decreases our trust in CI as a regression oracle.'\n\nTonight's evidence: the order-dependent leaked-logger flake (future-fibroid-hammerkop) cost four `Verified -1`s across changes 1715/1716/1717/1720, multiple recheck cycles, and hours of queued rebases before it was root-caused (deterministic RED via a two-file pytest invocation) and fixed class-wide in change 1721. Root-causing it once was cheaper than every recheck combined.\n\nThe guidance today points the other way. `AGENTS.md` says \"A `Verified -1` is a CI failure (open the run; comment `recheck` if it is a flake)\", and `CONTRIBUTING.md` §6 says \"**`Verified -1` but the failure looks transient/flaky.** Comment **`recheck`** to re-run CI on the same patchset.\" Both read as licence to retry a nondeterministic test until it passes. Adding the new rule without narrowing these two would leave the repo self-contradicting.\n\n## Approach\nOne authoritative wording in `AGENTS.md` (the canonical agent guidance, read by every harness); the two existing recheck lines are narrowed to point at it rather than restate it.\n\n1. **The rule, in `AGENTS.md`** — replace the parenthetical on the existing \"The gate\" bullet and add ONE sibling bullet in the same Git-workflow list, in that list's bolded-lead style. It must carry three things and no more: (a) a test that fails intermittently is a **bug to root-cause**, not a retry — debug it with the `/rebar-debug` workflow (hypothesis-driven, isolated worktree, deterministic RED before any fix) and fix the class, not the instance; (b) the one-sentence rationale — a flaky pipeline slows development, wastes tokens, and erodes CI as a regression oracle; (c) the narrow carve-out — `recheck` is for **provably environmental** faults (a TLS/promisor cert flake, a missing CI dispatch, a runner outage) and the recheck comment must state that reasoning. AGENTS.md's keep-it-lean rule caps this at the bullet plus the one-clause edit above it.\n2. **Reconcile `CONTRIBUTING.md` §6** — rewrite the \"`Verified -1` but the failure looks transient/flaky\" bullet so it splits the two cases explicitly: an environmental fault is recheckable with stated reasoning; a nondeterministic **test** is a bug and gets debugged, with a pointer to the AGENTS.md rule. The adjacent \"CI didn't run / no run appeared\" bullet is already an environmental case and stays as-is — it is the carve-out's worked example, so it is left alone deliberately rather than overlooked.\n3. **Cross-link, don't duplicate** — `docs/dependency-advisory-runbook.md` already states the same shape for its own gate (\"Only a DB-unreachable failure is recheckable. `recheck` can NEVER clear a real finding\"); it is consistent with the new rule and needs no edit. No new doc is created: `CONTRIBUTING.md` §6 remains the CI-triage home and now points at the rule instead of contradicting it.\n\n**Out of scope.** No change to the `recheck` trigger mechanism, the review bot, or `gerrit-verify.yaml` — this ticket changes guidance only. No quarantine/auto-retry tooling is added; the directive is explicitly that flakes are debugged, so building a retry facility would contradict it.\n\n## Scope\n- `AGENTS.md` — the rule bullet plus the narrowed clause on the existing \"The gate\" bullet.\n- `CONTRIBUTING.md` — §6's transient/flaky bullet, reconciled and cross-linked.\n- `tests/unit/test_flaky_test_guidance.py` — new; pins the rule and the reconciliation.\n\n## Testing\nThis is guidance, so the testable behavior is that the rule is present AND that the old retry-until-green licence is gone — the second half is what a plain \"text exists\" test would miss.\n- **Happy path:** a new `tests/unit/test_flaky_test_guidance.py` asserts `AGENTS.md` carries the rule's load-bearing phrases (intermittent failure is a bug to root-cause, the `/rebar-debug` pointer, the three-part rationale, and the environmental-only carve-out) and that `CONTRIBUTING.md` §6 names both branches.\n- **Failure/regression path:** the same test asserts the **contradiction is gone** — neither file still tells a reader to `recheck` because a failure \"looks transient/flaky\" without qualifying it as environmental. Verified by restoring the old sentence locally and watching the test fail before reverting.\n- **Gates:** `make lint`, `make typecheck`, the `check_*` gates, generator no-diff, and the CI unit selection (`test_codex_agent_guidance.py` and `test_agents_claim_assignee_guidance.py` already pin other AGENTS.md content, so they prove the edit did not disturb neighbouring guidance).\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] `AGENTS.md` carries the rule as concise agent guidance with the rationale compressed to one sentence naming all three costs (slows development, wastes tokens, erodes CI as a regression oracle).\n- [ ] The guidance names the deterministic-reproduction expectation via the `/rebar-debug` workflow and the narrow legitimate-recheck carve-out (provably environmental faults, reasoning stated in the recheck comment).\n- [ ] `CONTRIBUTING.md` §6 no longer offers `recheck` for a failure that merely \"looks transient/flaky\", and cross-links the AGENTS.md rule instead of restating it; `AGENTS.md`'s own \"`recheck` if it is a flake\" clause is narrowed the same way.\n- [ ] `tests/unit/test_flaky_test_guidance.py` pins both the new rule and the absence of the old licence, and passes; `make lint`, `make typecheck`, and the CI unit selection are green."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1786620815225170001,"uuid":"82808bd3-05e8-43a3-964b-afdb14c0e59a"}